# CP11 — sourcing and sensitivity. No changes to the model.
#
# Part A attached a public citation to every PARAMS and POLICY_PARAMS row that
# could carry one. Result: 0 SOURCED, 2 DERIVED, 185 ASSERTED, recorded in
# PRIORS.md under "Provenance" and summarised in SPEC §15.1. Three findings from
# the pass are recorded rather than fixed, because the world model is frozen for
# this checkpoint.
#
# Part B swept fourteen members at 0.25x-4x against OURS and B2 at 2,000 cases.
# Both conclusions survive the full 16x range for eleven of them; three lose the
# headline at 4x, and every one of those flips is B2 climbing rather than OURS
# falling. SPEC §15.2, out/sensitivity.json.

PRIORS.md
SPEC.md
settle/eval/sensitivity.py
tests/test_sensitivity.py
out/sensitivity.json
checkpoints/cp11.allowlist

# --- BLOCKED: one file outside this allowlist has to change ----------------
#
# `tests/test_ledger.py::test_EXE_1` asserts that `settle/execute/executor.py`
# and `settle/recon/reconcile.py` are the only modules under `settle/` that
# import `settle.sim.world`. `settle/eval/sensitivity.py` now does too, in
# `_rebind`, and it has to: `world.ACTION_LIFT` is built from PARAMS once at
# import, so a sweep that patched PARAMS and left it alone would report that
# `action_lift.*` — a REQUIRED member, upstream of every rupee in §14.4 — has no
# effect on anything. That is the most dangerous wrong answer this sweep can
# give, because a flat row is indistinguishable from a robust one.
#
# The suite is 656 passed, 1 failed, and the one failure is this and nothing
# else. It was left failing rather than fixed, because fixing it means editing a
# file this checkpoint may not touch, and the alternatives are worse:
#
#   * reaching the module through `sys.modules` or `importlib` would pass the
#     test by evading it. An unstated exception is how an invariant dies.
#   * restructuring the sweep to patch PARAMS in a fresh process before
#     `settle.sim.world` is first imported would work and needs no exception,
#     but it forces one process spawn per sweep point and makes the serial path
#     — the one the tests use — unable to sweep `action_lift.*` at all.
#
# The fix, if it is wanted, is one line and its comment:
#
#   -WORLD_READERS = {"settle/execute/executor.py", "settle/recon/reconcile.py"}
#   +# `settle/eval/sensitivity.py` rebinds `world.ACTION_LIFT` after patching
#   +# PARAMS. It reads the world's constants; it never dispatches.
#   +WORLD_READERS = {
#   +    "settle/execute/executor.py",
#   +    "settle/recon/reconcile.py",
#   +    "settle/eval/sensitivity.py",
#   +}
#
# That is the same kind of named exception `settle/recon/reconcile.py` already
# holds, for the same kind of reason: it reads the world rather than acting on
# it. Punya's call, not CC's.
